# Sermon Brainstorm Format

When a user asks to brainstorm a sermon or lesson idea (not yet write a full outline), organize the response or file using these sections in order:

## Required Sections

### Main Idea
- State the **Golden Apple** — one sharp, drivable sentence that is the central thesis
- Identify the **two or three layers** the message will move through (e.g., The Word vs. The Will)

### Main Points
- 3–4 teaching points, each containing:
  - A Scripture quote (NKJV)
  - A **bolded teaching point** sentence (no "Teaching Point:" label)
  - A **bolded discussion question** (no "Question:" label)

### Illustrations
- Named, vivid illustrations (personal story, biblical narrative, or metaphor)
- Each should map clearly to a teaching point
- Include the "cracked door" type of concrete image when available

### Other Scriptures
- Annotated list of supporting verses not used as primary texts
- One-line note after each explaining why it fits

### Possible Closing Questions
- 3–5 reflective questions designed to move from "What does the Bible say?" to "How will I live this?"

## File Naming
Brainstorm files follow the same convention as sermon files: `YYYY-MM-DD-slug.md` with full YAML frontmatter.
